---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../styles/suite.css";
const nightjarRepo = "https://github.com/nightwarelabz/nightjar";
---
<BaseLayout
  bare={true}
  title="BLACKSITE WEB"
  description="BLACKSITE WEB by NIGHTWARE LABZ: the nightjar Blacksite bench, rebuilt for the web. DNS, TLS, HTTP security grading, connect checks, domain recon, JWT and certificate inspection, hashes, codecs, subnet math, payloads, and a mirror that shows you what sites see. Public targets only. One knock, read, grade."
>
  <div class="bs">
    <span id="hero" hidden></span><span id="instrument" hidden></span><span id="skelli" hidden></span><span id="get-started" hidden></span><span id="lab" hidden></span><span id="about" hidden></span><span id="work" hidden></span><span id="current" hidden></span>
    <header class="bs-topbar">
      <a class="bs-brand" href="/bench/" aria-label="BLACKSITE WEB bench, home">
        <img src="/brand/nightware/nightjar-icon.png" alt="" width="34" height="34" />
        <span><span class="bs-name">BLACKSITE <em>WEB</em></span></span>
      </a>
      <span class="bs-edge-chip" id="edge-chip" role="status">EDGE …</span>
      <nav class="bs-top-links" aria-label="Lab">
        <a href="/" style="color:#ff3b47;font-weight:700">▸_ BLACKSITE OS</a>
        <a href={nightjarRepo}>nightjar for iPhone &amp; Mac ↗</a>
        <a href="/book/">Orange Book</a>
        <a href="/projects/orange/">Projects</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/nightwarelabz/nosuchmachine.net">Site source ↗</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <div class="bs-targetbar">
      <label for="global-target">TARGET</label>
      <input id="global-target" type="text" inputmode="url" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="example.com — a public host you own or are authorized to test" />
      <button class="bs-btn" id="battery-run" type="button">RUN FULL BATTERY</button>
      <p class="bs-doctrine"><strong>Doctrine: one knock, read, grade.</strong> This bench recons and grades — it does not exploit, spray, brute-force, or flood. Server-side probes refuse private, loopback, link-local, and cloud-metadata addresses before contact, and re-check every redirect. Test only systems you own or have permission to test. Local tools never leave your browser.</p>
      <p class="bs-doctrine" id="battery-status" role="status"></p>
    </div>

    <div class="bs-shell">
      <nav class="bs-rail" aria-label="Tools">
        <h2>CONSOLE</h2>
        <button class="rail-btn active" data-panel="overview" type="button">Overview <small>⌂</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="report" type="button">Report <small><span class="bs-report-badge" id="report-count">0</span></small></button>
        <h2>NETWORK — VIA EDGE</h2>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="dns" type="button">DNS <small>DoH ×3</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="tls" type="button">TLS Inspector <small>certs</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="http" type="button">HTTP Audit <small>EAZY grade</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="ports" type="button">Connect Check <small>TCP</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="recon" type="button">Domain Recon <small>RDAP·CT</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="mail" type="button">Email Security <small>SPF·DMARC</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="subs" type="button">Subdomains <small>CT census</small></button>
        <h2>LOCAL LAB — IN-BROWSER</h2>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="jwt" type="button">JWT Inspector <small>verify</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="vault" type="button">Vault <small>AES·hash</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="codecs" type="button">Codecs <small>b64·hex</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="subnet" type="button">Subnet Math <small>CIDR</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="cert" type="button">Cert Decoder <small>X.509</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="pqc" type="button">PQC Scanner <small>snoot</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="payload" type="button">Payload Bench <small>bytes</small></button>
        <button class="rail-btn" data-panel="mirror" type="button">Mirror <small>self-audit</small></button>
      </nav>

      <main class="bs-main" id="main">
        <section class="panel" id="panel-overview" aria-labelledby="ov-title">
          <div class="bs-hero">
            <div class="bs-hero-copy">
              <h1 id="ov-title">The bench is<br />the <em>website</em> now.</h1>
              <p class="bs-hero-sub">A working pentest bench in your browser — and an entire operating system one click past it.</p>
              <div class="bs-stats">
                <span><strong>17</strong> bench tools</span>
                <span><strong>636</strong> OS commands</span>
                <span><strong>207</strong> working verbs in the OS</span>
                <span><strong>3</strong> resolvers per DNS answer</span>
                <span><strong>0</strong> trackers — local tools stay local</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="bs-hero-art" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="bs-orbit"></span>
              <img src="/brand/nightware/nightjar-icon.png" alt="" width="300" height="300" />
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="bs-sub">This used to be a brochure for <a href={nightjarRepo}>nightjar</a>, the native network instrument for iPhone and Mac. Brochures don't pentest anything — so the site became the tool. Everything nightjar's Blacksite bench does from a phone, rebuilt for the browser, plus everything a browser and an edge network can honestly do. Set a target above and run the battery, or pick a tool. Need the native app? Build nightjar from source with Xcode on iPhone or Mac (iOS signing/provisioning applies) — <a href={`${nightjarRepo}/releases`}>releases</a> · <a href={`${nightjarRepo}#get-started`}>get started</a>.</p>
          <div class="bs-grid">
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="dns" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">NETWORK</span><strong>DNS</strong><span>A/AAAA/MX/TXT/CAA/DS and more, answered by Cloudflare, Google, and Quad9 side by side — spot the resolver that disagrees, check DNSSEC's AD flag.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="tls" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">NETWORK</span><strong>TLS Inspector</strong><span>A hand-rolled handshake reads the certificate chain as presented: SANs, validity, fingerprint, expiry findings, cipher suite.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="http" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">NETWORK</span><strong>HTTP Audit</strong><span>Redirect chain, header-by-header security grading (A–F), cookie flags, a CORS reflection probe, and a light fingerprint.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="ports" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">NETWORK</span><strong>Connect Check</strong><span>The honest TCP connect — open, closed, or silent — across the common service ports, from the edge's point of view.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="recon" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">NETWORK</span><strong>Domain Recon</strong><span>RDAP registration, the Certificate Transparency subdomain census, robots.txt, security.txt, sitemap — the public ledger, read properly.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="mail" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">NETWORK</span><strong>Email Security</strong><span>SPF posture, DMARC enforcement, DKIM selectors, MTA-STS — the anti-spoofing ledgers, graded A–F.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="subs" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">NETWORK</span><strong>Subdomain Census</strong><span>Every name that has ever appeared on a certificate for the domain — forgotten staging doors included.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="mirror" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">SELF-AUDIT</span><strong>Mirror</strong><span>Turn the bench around: what the edge sees of you, what WebRTC leaks, what your fingerprint says, and what Bluetooth devices around you offer.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="jwt" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">LOCAL</span><strong>JWT Inspector</strong><span>Decode, red-flag (alg=none, kid traversal, remote key URLs), and HMAC-verify — entirely on your machine.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="vault" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">LOCAL</span><strong>Vault</strong><span>SHA/MD5/HMAC hashing, file fingerprinting, AES-256-GCM sealing with PBKDF2, password strength and generation.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="codecs" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">LOCAL</span><strong>Codecs + Regex</strong><span>Base64/hex/URL/HTML/ROT13/binary, hexdump, entropy — and a regex bench with match groups.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="subnet" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">LOCAL</span><strong>Subnet Math</strong><span>CIDR, netmask, usable range, address class — including which ranges this bench itself refuses to probe, and why.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="cert" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">LOCAL</span><strong>Cert Decoder</strong><span>Paste a PEM and read it: names, SANs, serial, signature algorithm, SHA-256 fingerprint.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="pqc" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">LOCAL</span><strong>PQC Scanner</strong><span>snoot's front door: paste anything and find the RSA/ECC/DH that a quantum machine will open — with the NIST replacement named.</span></button>
            <button class="bs-toolcard" data-goto="payload" type="button"><span class="bs-tag">LOCAL</span><strong>Payload Bench</strong><span>The Send-tab habit, on the web: bytes in any encoding, out in every encoding, with CRC32, entropy, and SHA-256.</span></button>
          </div>
          <div class="bs-card" style="border-color:#8f1d24">
            <h3>▸_ BLACKSITE OS</h3>
            <p>The bench above is the toolkit. <strong><a href="/">BLACKSITE OS</a></strong> is the house it lives in: a windowed operating system in this tab — a real shell over a persistent virtual filesystem, the full 636-command Blacksite roster (every command live, edge-powered, or honestly named a limit), Python and JavaScript engines that run your programs, a vim-mannered editor, a process board, a gauntlet that tests the room itself, EMCON gating, themes, and generated wallpapers. It is the nightjar console, ported.</p>
            <p><a class="bs-btn" href="/" style="display:inline-block;text-decoration:none">BOOT BLACKSITE OS ▸</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="bs-card" style="border-color:#8f1d24">
            <h3><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" style="vertical-align:-2px" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4"/><path d="M12 3v2.4M12 18.6V21M3 12h2.4M18.6 12H21M5.6 5.6l1.7 1.7M16.7 16.7l1.7 1.7M18.4 5.6l-1.7 1.7M7.3 16.7l-1.7 1.7"/></svg> Daylight Bottle</h3>
            <p>Chase's age-encrypted message-in-a-bottle service (<a href="https://bottle.nosuchmachine.net">bottle.nosuchmachine.net</a>) now lives inside BLACKSITE OS: mint or load an age identity, seal bottles to keyring recipients, drop ciphertext at the service, and fetch and open your inbox — same wire protocol (<code>nsm.daylight-bottle.*.v1</code>), same crypto library, bottles sealed here open there and vice versa. Plaintext and private identities never leave the browser; the service stores ciphertext and public metadata only.</p>
            <p><a class="bs-btn" href="/#bottle" style="display:inline-block;text-decoration:none">OPEN THE BOTTLE DESK ▸</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>What a website honestly cannot do</h3>
            <p>No raw sockets, no SYN scans, no UDP, no packet capture, no sniffing your LAN — browsers don't offer them and this page won't pretend otherwise. Anything beyond connect-level truth needs the native instrument: nightjar on iPhone/Mac does BLE listening, imported-pcap dissection, protocol probes, listeners, and encrypted messaging that a web page structurally cannot. This bench is everything short of that line — done for real.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-dns" aria-labelledby="dns-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="dns-title">DNS</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">One question, three independent resolvers. Disagreement between them is the finding: split-horizon DNS, stale caches, and lame delegations all show up as a row that doesn't match.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="dns-name">NAME</label><input id="dns-name" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="example.com" /></div>
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="dns-type">TYPE</label><select id="dns-type"><option>A</option><option>AAAA</option><option>CNAME</option><option>MX</option><option>TXT</option><option>NS</option><option>SOA</option><option>CAA</option><option>PTR</option><option>SRV</option><option>DS</option><option>DNSKEY</option><option>HTTPS</option><option>ALL</option></select></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="dns-run" type="button">RESOLVE</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="dns-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <div class="bs-view" id="dns-view"></div>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="dns-out"></pre>
          <p class="bs-note">PTR works too — give an IP and ask for PTR. ALL sweeps the ten record types that matter most.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-tls" aria-labelledby="tls-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="tls-title">TLS Inspector</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">A hand-rolled ClientHello flies from the edge and the certificate flight is parsed byte by byte — the chain exactly as a client receives it, not as a database remembers it. TLS 1.3 encrypts its Certificate message; when that happens the inspector says so instead of inventing a chain.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="tls-host">HOST</label><input id="tls-host" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="example.com" /></div>
            <div class="bs-field" style="flex:0 1 120px"><label for="tls-port">PORT</label><input id="tls-port" type="number" value="443" min="1" max="65535" /></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="tls-run" type="button">INSPECT</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="tls-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <div class="bs-view" id="tls-view"></div>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="tls-out"></pre>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-http" aria-labelledby="http-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="http-title">HTTP Audit</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">One GET per redirect hop (each hop re-checked against the public-target guard), then everything graded: security headers scored A–F, every cookie's flags read, a foreign-Origin CORS probe, and a fingerprint from what the server volunteers.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="http-url">URL</label><input id="http-url" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="https://example.com/" /></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="http-run" type="button">AUDIT</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="http-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <div class="bs-view" id="http-view"></div>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="http-out"></pre>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-ports" aria-labelledby="tcp-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="tcp-title">Connect Check</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">nightjar's connect scan, from the edge: a full TCP connection either opens, is refused, or times out. No stealth claims — a connect is a knock, and the other side's logs will show it. Results are from a Cloudflare point of presence; a port can read differently from your network.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="tcp-host">HOST</label><input id="tcp-host" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="example.com" /></div>
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="tcp-ports">PORTS</label><input id="tcp-ports" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" value="common" placeholder="common, or 22,80,443" /></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="tcp-run" type="button">CHECK</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="tcp-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="tcp-out"></pre>
          <p class="bs-note">Custom lists are capped at 32 ports per check. This is a bench instrument, not a sweep cannon.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-recon" aria-labelledby="recon-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="recon-title">Domain Recon</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">Before touching a target, read what it already published. Registration (RDAP), every name it ever put on a certificate (Certificate Transparency via crt.sh), and its well-known public files — assembled into one picture.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="recon-domain">DOMAIN</label><input id="recon-domain" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="example.com" /></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="recon-run" type="button">RECON</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="recon-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="recon-out"></pre>
          <p class="bs-note">crt.sh is a volunteer-run ledger and rate-limits; a timeout there is their throttle, not your target hiding.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-mail" aria-labelledby="mail-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="mail-title">Email Security</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">Can anyone send mail as this domain? The anti-spoofing ledgers answer: SPF says who may send, DMARC says what to do with fakes, DKIM signs, MTA-STS enforces encrypted delivery. Graded like the EAZY battery grades them.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="mail-domain">DOMAIN</label><input id="mail-domain" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="example.com" /></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="mail-run" type="button">GRADE THE MAIL</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="mail-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="mail-out"></pre>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-subs" aria-labelledby="subs-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="subs-title">Subdomain Census</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">Every certificate ever issued for a domain is public, and each one names its hosts. This reads the ledger and lists them all — the attack surface the domain itself published.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="subs-domain">DOMAIN</label><input id="subs-domain" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="example.com" /></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="subs-run" type="button">RUN THE CENSUS</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="subs-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="subs-out"></pre>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-jwt" aria-labelledby="jwt-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="jwt-title">JWT Inspector</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">Decode the token, read its claims against the clock, red-flag the classic verifier traps (alg=none, kid traversal, remote key URLs, no expiry), and — for HMAC tokens — verify the signature against a secret you supply. Tokens can be credentials: this tool runs locally precisely so you can paste one safely.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="jwt-input">TOKEN</label><textarea id="jwt-input" spellcheck="false" placeholder="eyJhbGciOi…"></textarea></div>
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="jwt-secret">HMAC SECRET (OPTIONAL)</label><input id="jwt-secret" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="only needed to verify HS256/384/512" /></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="jwt-run" type="button">INSPECT</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="jwt-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="jwt-out"></pre>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-vault" aria-labelledby="vault-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="vault-title">Vault</h2>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>Hash lab</h3>
            <form class="bs-form">
              <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="hash-input">INPUT</label><textarea id="hash-input" spellcheck="false" placeholder="text, or hex in hex mode"></textarea></div>
              <div class="bs-field"><label for="hash-mode">INPUT IS</label><select id="hash-mode"><option value="text">text (UTF-8)</option><option value="hex">hex bytes</option></select></div>
              <div class="bs-field"><label for="hash-key">HMAC KEY (OPTIONAL)</label><input id="hash-key" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" /></div>
              <button class="bs-btn" id="hash-run" type="button">HASH</button>
              <label class="bs-btn ghost" for="hash-file" style="cursor:pointer">HASH A FILE…</label>
              <input id="hash-file" type="file" hidden />
            </form>
            <pre class="bs-out" id="hash-out"></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>AES-256-GCM seal</h3>
            <p>Encrypt text with a password — PBKDF2-SHA-256 at 310,000 iterations, authenticated encryption. The sealed block replaces the text; paste it back with the same password to open. Wrong password and tampered data fail identically, on purpose.</p>
            <form class="bs-form">
              <div class="bs-field"><label for="vault-password">PASSWORD</label><input id="vault-password" type="password" autocomplete="off" /></div>
              <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="vault-input">TEXT / SEALED BLOCK</label><textarea id="vault-input" spellcheck="false"></textarea></div>
              <button class="bs-btn" id="vault-seal" type="button">SEAL</button>
              <button class="bs-btn ghost" id="vault-open" type="button">OPEN</button>
            </form>
            <pre class="bs-out" id="vault-out"></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>Passwords</h3>
            <form class="bs-form">
              <div class="bs-field"><label for="pw-input">CHECK A PASSWORD (LOCALLY)</label><input id="pw-input" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" /></div>
              <button class="bs-btn ghost" id="pw-check" type="button">CHECK</button>
              <div class="bs-field" style="flex:0 1 120px"><label for="pwgen-length">GEN LENGTH</label><input id="pwgen-length" type="number" value="20" min="8" max="128" /></div>
              <button class="bs-btn" id="pwgen-run" type="button">GENERATE</button>
              <div class="bs-field"><label for="pwgen-out">GENERATED</label><input id="pwgen-out" type="text" readonly /></div>
            </form>
            <pre class="bs-out" id="pwgen-meta"></pre>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-codecs" aria-labelledby="codec-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="codec-title">Codecs + Regex</h2>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>Convert</h3>
            <form class="bs-form">
              <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="codec-input">INPUT</label><textarea id="codec-input" spellcheck="false"></textarea></div>
              <div class="bs-field"><label for="codec-op">OPERATION</label><select id="codec-op"><option value="b64e">Base64 encode</option><option value="b64d">Base64 decode</option><option value="b64url">Base64URL encode</option><option value="hexe">Hex encode</option><option value="hexd">Hex decode</option><option value="urle">URL encode</option><option value="urld">URL decode</option><option value="htmle">HTML escape</option><option value="htmld">HTML unescape</option><option value="rot13">ROT13</option><option value="bin">Binary</option><option value="hexdump">Hexdump</option><option value="entropy">Entropy analysis</option></select></div>
              <button class="bs-btn" id="codec-run" type="button">RUN</button>
              <button class="bs-btn ghost" id="codec-swap" type="button">OUTPUT → INPUT</button>
              <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="codec-output">OUTPUT</label><textarea id="codec-output" spellcheck="false" readonly></textarea></div>
            </form>
          </div>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>Regex bench</h3>
            <form class="bs-form">
              <div class="bs-field"><label for="regex-pattern">PATTERN</label><input id="regex-pattern" type="text" spellcheck="false" placeholder="[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+" /></div>
              <div class="bs-field" style="flex:0 1 110px"><label for="regex-flags">FLAGS</label><input id="regex-flags" type="text" value="g" spellcheck="false" /></div>
              <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="regex-subject">SUBJECT</label><textarea id="regex-subject" spellcheck="false"></textarea></div>
              <button class="bs-btn" id="regex-run" type="button">MATCH</button>
            </form>
            <pre class="bs-out" id="regex-out"></pre>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-subnet" aria-labelledby="subnet-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="subnet-title">Subnet Math</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">The arithmetic under every scope document. The class field tells you which space an address lives in — and the private, loopback, and link-local classes are exactly the ones this bench's edge probes refuse to touch.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="subnet-input">ADDRESS / CIDR</label><input id="subnet-input" type="text" spellcheck="false" placeholder="192.168.1.10/24 or 2001:db8::1/64" /></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="subnet-run" type="button">CALCULATE</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="subnet-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="subnet-out"></pre>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-cert" aria-labelledby="cert-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="cert-title">Certificate Decoder</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">Paste a certificate — PEM, base64 DER, or hex — and read it with a hand-rolled ASN.1 parser. The local twin of the TLS Inspector, for certs you already hold.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="cert-input">CERTIFICATE</label><textarea id="cert-input" spellcheck="false" placeholder="-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----"></textarea></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="cert-run" type="button">DECODE</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="cert-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="cert-out"></pre>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-pqc" aria-labelledby="pqc-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="pqc-title">PQC Scanner</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">Paste a config, an SSH banner, a TLS report, a code fragment — anything with cryptography in it. The scanner (a web cut of <a href="https://github.com/nightwarelabz/snoot">snoot</a>) flags classical algorithms and names the NIST post-quantum replacement for each. Harvest-now-decrypt-later is the threat: ciphertext recorded today opens the day a capable quantum machine exists.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="pqc-input">TEXT TO SCAN</label><textarea id="pqc-input" spellcheck="false" placeholder="e.g. output of ssh -Q key, an sshd_config, a TLS cipher list, an openssl s_client transcript…"></textarea></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="pqc-run" type="button">SCAN FOR CLASSICAL CRYPTO</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="pqc-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="pqc-out"></pre>
          <p class="bs-note">Runs entirely in your browser — nothing you paste leaves the page. The full snoot walks codebases and emits CBOM (CycloneDX); this is its front door.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-payload" aria-labelledby="payload-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="payload-title">Payload Bench</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">Build the bytes before you send them anywhere: enter text, hex, or base64 and get every representation back with length, CRC32, entropy, and SHA-256 — the same discipline as nightjar's Send tab, where you see both sides of an exchange before it happens.</p>
          <form class="bs-form">
            <div class="bs-field"><label for="payload-format">INPUT IS</label><select id="payload-format"><option value="text">text (UTF-8)</option><option value="hex">hex</option><option value="base64">base64</option></select></div>
            <div class="bs-field wide"><label for="payload-input">PAYLOAD</label><textarea id="payload-input" spellcheck="false" placeholder="GET / HTTP/1.1"></textarea></div>
            <button class="bs-btn" id="payload-run" type="button">BUILD</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="payload-out" type="button">Copy</button>
          </form>
          <pre class="bs-out" id="payload-out"></pre>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-mirror" aria-labelledby="mirror-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="mirror-title">Mirror</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">Every probe on this bench can be turned around. This is your own EMCON picture: what this server saw, what your browser volunteers, what WebRTC hands to any page, and what the radios around you are offering.</p>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>Edge echo</h3>
            <p>Your request, as the edge received it — headers, TLS version and cipher, network identity. Anything here is visible to every site you visit.</p>
            <div class="bs-btnrow"><button class="bs-btn" id="echo-run" type="button">SHOW ME</button><button class="bs-btn ghost" data-copy="echo-out" type="button">Copy</button></div>
            <pre class="bs-out" id="echo-out"></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>WebRTC exposure</h3>
            <p>WebRTC's ICE gathering shows pages addresses your browser would rather keep quiet. Run it and see what leaks — an mDNS name is the mask working; a raw LAN or public address is exposure.</p>
            <div class="bs-btnrow"><button class="bs-btn" id="webrtc-run" type="button">RUN CHECK</button></div>
            <pre class="bs-out" id="webrtc-out"></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>Fingerprint surface</h3>
            <p>The quiet identifiers: cores, memory, screen, timezone, languages, automation flags. Individually dull; together, a name.</p>
            <div class="bs-btnrow"><button class="bs-btn" id="fingerprint-run" type="button">READ SURFACE</button></div>
            <pre class="bs-out" id="fingerprint-out"></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="bs-card">
            <h3>Bluetooth</h3>
            <p>Web Bluetooth (Chrome/Edge) can ask a nearby BLE device what it is and what services it offers — read-only here, nothing is ever written. nightjar's native listening post does this passively and continuously; a web page gets one polite question at a time.</p>
            <div class="bs-btnrow"><button class="bs-btn" id="ble-run" type="button">ASK A DEVICE</button></div>
            <pre class="bs-out" id="ble-out"></pre>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="panel" id="panel-report" aria-labelledby="report-title" hidden>
          <h2 class="bs-h" id="report-title">Report</h2>
          <p class="bs-sub">Every probe you run lands here, in order. Export it as JSON or Markdown before you close the page — the bench keeps nothing server-side, mirroring nightjar's rule that the evidence belongs to the operator.</p>
          <div class="bs-btnrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">
            <button class="bs-btn" id="report-export-md" type="button">EXPORT MARKDOWN</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" id="report-export-json" type="button">EXPORT JSON</button>
            <button class="bs-btn ghost" id="report-clear" type="button">CLEAR</button>
          </div>
          <div id="report-list"></div>
        </section>
      </main>
    </div>

    <footer class="bs-foot">
      <span>BLACKSITE WEB · NIGHTWARE LABZ</span>
      <span>Doctrine: one knock, read, grade. Public targets only.</span>
